    "## Part 1: Why Pandas?\n",
    "\n",
    "With a Python list or NumPy array, data has no labels — you access it by position. If you have student data in an array, you have to remember that column 0 is name, column 1 is age, column 2 is GPA. This is fragile and confusing.\n",
    "\n",
    "Pandas assigns **labels** to both rows and columns. You access data by name, not by position. This makes your code readable, your analysis clear, and your errors much easier to catch.\n",
    "\n",
    "Beyond labels, Pandas gives you:\n",
    "- Built-in handling of missing values\n",
    "- Tools to load data from CSV, Excel, JSON, and databases\n",
    "- Powerful grouping, filtering, and aggregation\n",
    "- Integration with Matplotlib for visualisation\n",
    "- The ability to work with datasets of millions of rows efficiently\n",
    "\n",
    "By convention, Pandas is always imported as `pd`:"
   ]
